// sin(k*pi/1024) and cos(k*pi/1024) for k = 1..256, four doubles each.
//
// One table serves every cascade width. A correctly rounded expansion stays a valid,
// correctly rounded expansion when you drop its trailing limbs, so dd_cascade reads the
// first two doubles, td_cascade the first three, qd_cascade all four. The generator
// verifies each prefix against mpmath: 32, 48 and 64 decimal digits respectively.
//
// The values are the ones the qd sincos tables carry (Bailey/Hida), so qd and qd_cascade
// evaluate sin and cos from identical constants.
//
// Why pi/1024 and not the pi/16 the double-double path uses: the Taylor series is
// evaluated on the reduced argument, and a 15-entry inverse-factorial table only reaches
// quad-double precision if that argument is below pi/2048. Reducing to pi/32 leaves a
// series that would need terms out to 1/65! -- which is why the cascade types capped at
// double-double accuracy no matter how wide they were (universal#1318).

